            "description": "Watch Panama vs Croatia highlights and goals from World Cup 2026 on ReFooty: Football Highlights Hub. Match played on June 25, 2026. Under the bright lights of the World Cup , Panama and Croatia squared off in a clash of styles that promised intensity and tactical intrigue from the opening whistle. Both sides entered the pitch on June 25, 2026 , knowing that every moment could define their tournament journey. A Tactical Stalemate in the Opening Half The match began with Croatia attempting to dictate the tempo through possession, but Panama proved to be an organized and resilient opponent. As early as the 2nd minute, Luka Modric saw a header go wide following a cross from Marco Pasalic . Panama responded through Amir Murillo , whose early effort was blocked by a desperate Croatian defense. While the Europeans controlled much of the ball, clear-cut chances were a premium. Just before the interval, Martin Baturina came inches away from breaking the deadlock with a powerful strike from distance that whistled past the post. Budimir's Instant Impact Recognizing the need for a clinical edge, the Croatian side introduced Ante Budimir at the start of the second half. The substitution paid immediate dividends in the 54th minute. After a well-placed cross from Josip Stanisic , Budimir found space in the six-yard box to fire home a left-footed shot into the bottom corner. The goal altered the complexion of the match, forcing Panama to abandon their defensive shape in search of an equalizer. Panama Pressures Until the Final Whistle Refusing to back down, Panama launched a series of dangerous attacks. José Fajardo and Carlos Harvey both missed narrow opportunities with headers, while Dominik Livakovic was forced into a crucial save from a low Amir Murillo drive in the 68th minute. As the clock ticked down, tensions flared, resulting in yellow cards for Yoel Bárcenas and Petar Sucic . In the final moments of stoppage time, Murillo had one last chance with a direct free kick, but his effort soared over the bar, leaving Croatia to celebrate a hard-earned victory.",
